<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aleijadinho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1738–1814</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Antônio Francisco Lisboa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aleijadinho, born Antônio Francisco Lisboa, was a sculptor and architect of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minas-gerais">Minas Gerais</a> and the leading artist of the Brazilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>. The son of a Portuguese architect and an enslaved African woman, he worked on churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouro-preto">Ouro Preto</a> and other mining towns. Despite a disabling illness, he carved the soapstone prophets at the sanctuary of Congonhas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleijadinho.jpg" alt="Aleijadinho" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Euclásio Ventura · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suposto_retrato_de_Aleijadinho_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
